# ── Backend API ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The onboardos-backend origin. Used both server-side (route handlers,
# server components) and client-side (direct browser -> API calls for
# business data, once bootstrapped — see lib/auth's architecture note).
# Must be in the backend's CORS_ORIGINS for the client-side calls to work.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── This app's own public URL ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Used for absolute URLs in metadata, OG image generation, sitemap.xml,
# and robots.txt — none of these can safely rely on a relative path.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3001

# No cookie-encryption secret is needed here: the httpOnly refresh-token
# cookie holds the backend's own opaque, high-entropy, server-hashed
# bearer token verbatim (see lib/auth/session.ts) — it is not a JWT with
# a client-readable payload, so there is nothing here to encrypt or sign
# beyond what httpOnly + Secure + SameSite already provide.

# ── Error reporting (Sentry) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# All optional — every file that reads these (src/instrumentation.ts,
# instrumentation-client.ts, sentry.*.config.ts, next.config.ts) is
# designed to no-op safely when they're unset, verified against the
# installed @sentry/nextjs package's own source, not assumed. Leave
# this whole section blank to build and run with no Sentry account at
# all; nothing else needs to change.
#
# A Sentry DSN is meant to be public — the same way a Stripe
# *publishable* key is public and only the *secret* key is sensitive —
# so this is intentionally NEXT_PUBLIC_, not a server-only secret.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0
#
# The three below are build-time only (sourcemap upload), never shipped
# to the browser — find them in Sentry → Settings → Auth Tokens (needs
# project:releases scope) and your org/project slugs from the project
# URL. Omit SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN and the build just skips the upload step
# rather than failing.
# SENTRY_ORG=your-org-slug
# SENTRY_PROJECT=your-project-slug
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ── Contact form delivery (Resend) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Also all optional — api/contact/route.ts only constructs a Resend
# client when RESEND_API_KEY is set (confirmed from the installed
# `resend` package's own source: its constructor throws if no key is
# found anywhere, so this app checks first rather than ever calling
# `new Resend()` unguarded). With none of this set, the route keeps its
# original behavior: every submission is still validated, honeypot-
# checked, and logged server-side — it just isn't emailed anywhere.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_your_api_key
#
# Must be an address on a domain verified in your Resend dashboard
# (Resend → Domains) — an unverified `from` domain fails per-request,
# not at startup, and that failure is caught and logged, not thrown.
# CONTACT_FORM_FROM_EMAIL=hello@yourdomain.com
#
# Where submissions actually land. The submitter's own address is set
# as the email's Reply-To, so replying from this inbox goes straight
# back to them.
# CONTACT_FORM_TO_EMAIL=sales@yourdomain.com
